---
import Lesson from "../../layouts/Lesson.astro";
import Mixup from "../../components/Mixup.astro";
import Scenario from "../../components/Scenario.astro";
import { apiSees } from "../../data/learn/api-sees";
import type { CheckQuestion } from "../../data/learn/types";

const checks: CheckQuestion[] = [
  {
    id: "bob",
    question:
      "Bob asks the agent for Alice’s calendar. The agent uses its own API key for every user. What does the calendar API know about who is asking?",
    options: [
      {
        text: "That it is Bob, because Bob is the one who typed the request.",
        why: "Bob typed it to the agent. The API never sees the conversation, only the request the agent sends.",
      },
      {
        text: "Nothing. It knows which software is calling, and no more.",
        correct: true,
        why: "The key is the agent’s, and it is the same key whoever is asking. So the API cannot keep Bob out of Alice’s calendar by itself. If the agent adds a name to the request, the API is being told who is asking, not shown.",
      },
      {
        text: "That it is Alice, because it is her calendar.",
        why: "Whose calendar is asked for says nothing about who asked. That is the gap Bob is using.",
      },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "logs",
    question:
      "Alice makes an API key in her calendar settings and gives it to an agent. Later a meeting is deleted. What do the API’s logs say?",
    options: [
      {
        text: "That Alice deleted it.",
        correct: true,
        why: "The key is hers, and nothing in it says that software was involved. Whatever the agent does with it is recorded as her doing it.",
      },
      {
        text: "That the agent deleted it for Alice.",
        why: "Nothing in a key Alice made names the agent. The API has no record that an agent exists.",
      },
      {
        text: "That the calendar-assistant app deleted it.",
        why: "That is what a token would let the API record. Alice’s key names her account, and no app.",
      },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "evidence",
    question:
      "Which of these tells the API the request is for Alice, without it having to take the agent’s word?",
    options: [
      {
        text: "A header the agent adds: `X-User: alice`.",
        why: "The API can read that header, and apply its rules to the name in it. But the agent wrote it, and can write any name there. So can anyone else who has the agent’s key.",
      },
      {
        text: "A line in the agent’s prompt: “only access the current user’s data.”",
        why: "A prompt instructs the model. The API never sees it, and text the agent reads later can talk it out of following it.",
      },
      {
        text: "A token that names Alice, signed by a service the API trusts.",
        correct: true,
        why: "The agent did not write the token and cannot alter it without breaking the signature. The API is relying on the service that signed it, not on the agent. The next lesson shows how that works.",
      },
    ],
  },
];
---

<Lesson
  slug="what-the-api-sees"
  lede="You can see the whole conversation between Alice and your agent. The API at the other end cannot. Everything about agent authorization follows from what it can see."
  checks={checks}
  keep={[
    "An API sees a request and a **credential**. It does not see the user, the conversation or the intent.",
    "A credential is worth what it proves: **who is acting, for whom, and who said they could.**",
    "The agent’s own key proves the first. A key the user made proves the second. A token the user approved can carry all three.",
    { rule: "Never ask a user to paste a secret into the conversation. Anything typed there, the model can be asked to repeat." },
    "A limit only counts where the agent cannot talk its way around it. That place is the API.",
  ]}
  names={[
    ["What a request carries as evidence", "Credential"],
    ["The agent’s own secret string", "API key"],
    ["Your agent, as the software asking for access", "Client"],
    ["The calendar service", "Resource server"],
  ]}
>
  <h2>Sit where the API sits</h2>
  <p>
    Alice types a question into your agent: <em>what’s on my calendar tomorrow?</em> The agent decides to call the
    calendar API. From where you sit, nothing could be clearer. Alice asked, and the agent is fetching Alice’s events
    for Alice.
  </p>
  <p>
    Now sit where the calendar API sits. It receives an HTTP request. It cannot see Alice. It cannot see the
    conversation, the model or your code. It sees the request, and whatever came attached to it.
  </p>
  <p>
    That attachment is called a <strong>credential</strong>, and it is the only evidence the API has. A credential
    can prove up to three things:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Who is acting?</strong> Which piece of software sent this request.</li>
    <li><strong>For whom?</strong> Which person it is working for, if any.</li>
    <li><strong>Who said they could?</strong> What was approved, and by whom.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Try three credentials</h2>
  <p>
    Here are three things your agent could attach. Before you try them, make a guess: which of them lets the API tell
    a request for Alice from a request for Bob?
  </p>

  <Scenario scenario={apiSees} glance="All three at a glance" />

  <h2>Where the two keys fall short</h2>
  <p>
    An API key is what most of us reach for first. For a program with one owner it can be enough, as long as the key
    is kept where the model cannot read it and can do no more than the job needs. The trouble starts when one agent
    works for many people. One key is one identity, so every request looks the same to the API, whoever asked. The
    agent can add a name to the request, and the API can apply its rules to that name. But a name the sender typed is
    a claim, not evidence: the API is still taking the agent’s word for whose request this is.
  </p>
  <p>
    In an agent that name is often filled in by the model, so text the model has read can choose whose data is
    fetched. Where an API offers nothing but a key, your own code has to set the user from its signed-in session,
    never from anything the model wrote, and check each request against that user before the key is used.
  </p>
  <p>
    Having Alice make a key of her own and hand it over fails the other way. The API now knows whose account this
    is, and nothing else. It cannot tell your agent from Alice herself, or from anything else that holds the key,
    so everything the agent does is recorded as her doing it. Most such keys open the whole account and last until
    someone remembers to delete them.
  </p>
  <p>
    And Alice had to copy a secret by hand. If she pasted it into the conversation, the model has read it, and
    anything the model has read it can be asked to repeat. So never ask a user to paste a secret into a
    conversation: not a key, not a token, and above all not a password, which the agent must never see at all.{' '}
    <a href="/learn/getting-a-token/">Lesson 3</a> is about how that is arranged.
  </p>
  <p>
    Both failures matter more for an agent than for an ordinary program, because an agent can be talked into things. It reads
    web pages, emails and documents, and any of them may contain text written to steer it. If the agent’s key can
    read everyone’s calendar, one successful trick reads everyone’s calendar.
  </p>
  <p>
    So one rule runs through this whole guide. A limit that lives in the agent, or in its prompt, is a suggestion. A
    limit the API enforces, using evidence the agent cannot make up, is a control.
  </p>

  <Mixup belief="The API knows which user this is, because my agent knows.">
    <p>
      What your agent knows stays in your agent. The API learns only what the credential proves. If the credential
      is the same for every user, the API has only your agent’s word for which user this is.
    </p>
  </Mixup>

  <h2>What the token changes</h2>
  <p>
    The third credential is a different kind of thing. The agent did not write it. A service the API already trusts
    wrote it and signed it, after asking Alice. It says who it is for, which app it was given to and what it allows,
    and the API can check all of that without taking the agent’s word for anything.
  </p>
  <p>
    How a few lines of text manage that is the next lesson. One caution to carry with you: a key and a token share a
    weakness. Each works for whoever holds a copy. The last lesson comes back to that.
  </p>
</Lesson>
